{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 15,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.012,
            0.837,
            0.05
          ],
          "content": "Chapter 7 — Particulate Nature of Matter"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.888,
            0.029,
            0.923,
            0.045
          ],
          "content": "113"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.094,
            0.26,
            0.115
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Snapshots"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.128,
            0.612,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": "Matter is composed of extremely small particles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.147,
            0.785,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": "The particles are held together by interparticle forces of attractions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.165,
            0.801,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": "The interparticle attractions are the strongest in solids, a little weaker in liquids, and the weakest in gases."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.79,
            0.056,
            0.928,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.2,
            0.803,
            0.251
          ],
          "content": "Solids have a fixed shape and size due to strong interparticle attraction, minimum interparticle space, and no free movement of the constituent particles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.251,
            0.803,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "The interparticle attraction in liquids is slightly weaker than in solids, enabling the particles to move within a particular space and providing them with a little more interparticle spacing. Therefore, liquids have a definite volume but no fixed shape."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.32,
            0.804,
            0.39
          ],
          "content": "The interparticle attractions in gases are negligible, making their particles completely free to move from one place to another and resulting in maximum interparticle space. Therefore, gases have no fixed shape and volume."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.414,
            0.392,
            0.436
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Keep the curiosity alive"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.448,
            0.415,
            0.468
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "1. Choose the correct option."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.469,
            0.743,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": "The primary difference between solids and liquids is that the constituent particles are:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.508,
            0.743,
            0.545
          ],
          "content": "(i) closely packed in solids, while they are stationary in liquids."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.545,
            0.707,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": "(ii) far apart in solids and have fixed position in liquids."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.564,
            0.743,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": "(iii) always moving in solids and have fixed position in liquids."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.6,
            0.742,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": "(iv) closely packed in solids and move past each other in liquids."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.639,
            0.743,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": "2. Which of the following statements are true? Correct the false statements."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.679,
            0.743,
            0.716
          ],
          "content": "(i) Melting ice into water is an example of the transformation of a solid into a liquid."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.716,
            0.743,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Melting process involves a decrease in interparticle attractions during the transformation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.753,
            0.642,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Solids have a fixed shape and a fixed volume."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.772,
            0.743,
            0.81
          ],
          "content": "(iv) The interparticle interactions in solids are very strong, and the interparticle spaces are very small."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.585,
            0.832,
            0.957,
